hello everybody my name's lloyd  essler i've always had a bit of an  interest in cemeteries and  dead things and interesting stories  around early south and and if you want  to come along  to the eastern cemetery 6 30 on  the 4th of november having a bit of a  walk around here for  about an hour and looking at some of the  interesting graves  and a few of the stories from some of  the southern pioneers so  everybody welcome to come along to that  we're just standing beside a grave here  this is the  mcnab grave and  uh one of the early ones here so it's  alexander and janet mcnabb but the  the um this is the tomb also of robert  mcnabb who is a member of parliament  and uh well known as the author  of a couple of very important books on  south and one of them is the old whaling  days  and murie heku which was very valuable  bit of  early research robert was the  survivor of a family  he was the oldest child while he was  away at school  um this is in 1871 all his siblings died  they caught a disease and within a few  days there was  there's four four or five children all  died so he ended up being the only child  one of the tragedies of the  early years but not particularly  uncommon because medical  practice was a bit primitive there were  lots of  dangerous things a lot of people got  drowned in those days and of course  there was infectious diseases so  um glad we got one survivor out of the  mcnabb family to make a contribution to  south and history
 

